Currently, it's possible to use `jsr:`, `npm:` and relative paths to lint plugins. But it's not possible to install a npm/jsr package, and use the installed package name in `imports` in `"plugins"`.

The drawback here is mostly it becomes less clear if the specific version for the lint plugin in `"imports"` is respected, or if the latest version is used when using `jsr:@scope/<package>`.


## Reproduction

1. Run the snippet below (e.g. via `deno eval`)
2. Use `deno lint` to re-run linting after creating files

```ts
await Deno.writeTextFile('./main.ts', `
const test = 42;
console.log(test);
`);

await Deno.writeTextFile('./deno.json', `
{
  "lock": false,
  "lint": {
    "plugins": ["csvn/lint"]
  },
  "imports": {
    "csvn": "jsr:@csvn/jsr-test@^0.0.3"
  }
}
`);

await new Deno.Command(Deno.execPath(), {
  args: ['lint'],
  cwd: Deno.cwd(),
  stdout: 'inherit',
  stderr: 'inherit'
}).output();
```

The above should result in the error message:

```
error: Module not found "<path_to_cwd>/csvn/lint"
```

Changing the above to `"plugins": ["jsr:@csvn/jsr-test/lint"]` will work as intended.

Many ESLint rules require some form of configuration to work. And to create re-usable Lint rules for frameworks (e.g. `fresh`), they will likely require passing options. This will open up a lot more possibilities when creating lint rules.

## Suggestion

Allow passing options instead of pure strings to `"include"`, similar to ESLint. Given that ESLint uses an object for it's `rules`, and not an array like `"rules.include"`, the exact same API may not work.

```jsonc
// deno.json
{
  "lint": {
    "plugins": [
      "jsr:@fresh/lint",
      "jsr:@foo/lint"
    ],
    "rules": {
      "include": [
         // Setting the Island directory
         ["fresh/island-imports", "src/islands"],

         // Custom ESLint "no-restricted-globals" rule
         ["foo/no-restricted-globals", "event", "window"],
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

And options are then passed to custom plugins in the `Context.options` property (compatible with ESLint).

```ts
const plugin: Deno.lint.Plugin = {
  name: "foo",
  rules: {
    "no-restricted-globals": {
      create(context) {
        context.options; // Passed via deno.json
        // ["event", "window"]
        return { ... };
      },
    },
  },
};
export default plugin;
```

